 "default": "ac",
 "device": "csr310",
 "gaps": [
  {
   "because": "needs-drawing",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "rear",
    "top",
    "bottom",
    "left",
    "right"
   ],
   "wanted": "A figure of any face but the front. Neither document draws one, and on THIS device that is a smaller hole than usual: the datasheet's own \"All ports and PSUs are accessible at the front\" means the rear carries no user-facing hardware but the grounding point. The quick start's step 3 pictures that point twice, once on the rear panel and once on the side, as a two-hole lug with the earth symbol beside it - enough to know it exists on both, not enough to place either, so neither is placed. The SIDE panels carry the intake and exhaust on a side-to-side box and are drawn nowhere.",
   "what": "rear-elevation"
  },
  {
   "because": "needs-photo",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "front"
   ],
   "wanted": "What the component at 239.8-260.8 is. It is 21.0 x 3.8 in the silkscreen band between the SFP+ numerals and the AS7315-27X model text, and it reads as eight cells with the outer two red and the inner six pale. Neither document mentions it: the fourteen callouts do not point at it, the specification lists no display and no switches, and the quick start's step 5 figure - which is otherwise the most legible thing in the corpus - is cropped just short of it. A stack-identity display would fit the device, which stacks two units and has a STACK-SYNC and a STACK-CTRL port; so would a DIP bank. It is drawn as a plain bezel with no claim about which, and `common/seven-segment-dual@1` was NOT used, because reaching for a part whose name asserts what the thing is would be the guess this gap exists to avoid.",
   "what": "stack-id-indicator"
  },
  {
   "because": "needs-photo",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "front"
   ],
   "wanted": "The module letters on the two-high QSFP28 block at 322.5-342.5. The quick start names M0 (the single 100G cage) and M1 (the SFP28 group) outright, and the datasheet elevation carries a `M? 100GBASE-R` label over this block that does not resolve at 8.15 px/mm - it could be M2 or M3, and the block holds two ports whose own numbering is unread. They are modelled as `stack-0` and `stack-1` from the datasheet's callout 11, \"2 x 100G QSFP28 stacking ports\", which is a function rather than a name.",
   "what": "stacking-qsfp-labels"
  },
  {
   "because": "vendor-silent",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "front"
   ],
   "wanted": "What the six lamps in the stacking block's bezel do. Their positions are measured - 325.1, 327.5, 330.4, 334.3, 337.5, 339.7 - and two of the six are visibly larger than the other four, which usually means a port lamp beside a set of lane lamps. Nothing in either document says. Four lane lamps and two port lamps is the shape the AGR110 uses, but that is a pattern, not a reading, so the six are placed and left unassigned.",
   "what": "stacking-block-lamps"
  },
  {
   "because": "vendor-silent",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "front"
   ],
   "wanted": "How many fans are in the tray. The datasheet says \"Hot swappable redundant fan modules\" - plural, inside one tray - and the FRU table sells exactly one \"Fan Tray Spare Part\", FAN-1U-1x1J-S. The elevation shows a closed tray with no grille and no seams. So the tray is one module with no nested bays and the redundancy is stated in attrs without a count, rather than a number being invented to fill the field.",
   "what": "fan-count"
  },
  {
   "because": "vendor-silent",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "front"
   ],
   "wanted": "A wattage for either supply. The datasheet's \"Power Capacity: 300 W (maximum)\" and the quick start's \"193 Watts maximum\" are both the CHASSIS's - one a capacity, one a consumption - and neither is an output rating for a module. Input ranges are published for both variants and are carried on the parts; the output is not, so L27 stands on both and the hole stays visible.",
   "what": "psu-output-rating"
  },
  {
   "because": "vendor-silent",
   "blocks": [],
   "kind": "declared",
   "scope": [
    "front"
   ],
   "wanted": "An LED table. Neither document has one. Six system lamps are named on the faceplate - LOC, DIAG, PSU1, PSU2, FAN, ALRM - and the quick start states one colour for four of them: \"After power on, the PSU1/PSU2, DIAG, ALRM, and FAN LEDs should all be on green.\" That is more than the CSR200 gives and still not a vocabulary, so every lamp carries `off` and one green `on`.",
   "what": "led-vocabularies"
  },
  {
   "because": "L29",
   "blocks": [],
   "count": 1,
   "kind": "derived",
   "wanted": "a `power-draw-max-w` on each module this chassis accepts, from the vendor's own per-card table - Cisco publishes one per ambient, most vendors publish none, and until they land a module total is a floor",
   "what": "module-power"
